      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bat Yam was founded in 1919 as Bayit VeGan by a Mizrachi-affiliated homeowners association.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bat_Yam",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city began as a suburban development called Bayit ve-Gan in 1926, just south of the historic port of Jaffa.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Bat-Yam",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 2011, two new railway stations opened, making Bat Yam the terminus for both the Tel Aviv–Rishon LeZion West line and the new Yoseftal line.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bat_Yam",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Yehuda Abarbanel Mental Health Center, named after a medieval Portuguese rabbi, was established by British Mandate authorities in 1944.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bat_Yam",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Bat Yam’s Heritage Museum, housed in the library building, displays photographs and documents from 1926 to 1948, including details of the city’s role in Israel’s War of Independence.",
